1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:05,092
What if the biggest pricing mistake isn't a wrong number,

2
00:00:05,142 --> 00:00:08,519
but comparing two things that were never

3
00:00:08,569 --> 00:00:11,200
equivalent in the first place?

4
00:00:12,450 --> 00:00:15,698
A publishing catalogue looks simple: a title,

5
00:00:15,748 --> 00:00:17,444
a series and a price.

6
00:00:17,494 --> 00:00:21,260
But behind every title sit formats, levels, editions,

7
00:00:21,310 --> 00:00:23,200
packs and access rights.

8
00:00:24,450 --> 00:00:27,624
For this case study I generated a fictional catalogue:

9
00:00:27,674 --> 00:00:30,740
three hundred and sixty-two products, twelve series,

10
00:00:30,790 --> 00:00:33,051
and data problems planted on purpose.

11
00:00:33,101 --> 00:00:35,200
No real publisher, no real prices.

12
00:00:36,450 --> 00:00:41,239
First, I recovered structure from the titles, without guessing.

13
00:00:41,289 --> 00:00:44,675
If a title doesn't mention access duration,

14
00:00:44,725 --> 00:00:47,200
that's unknown, not unlimited.

15
00:00:48,450 --> 00:00:50,792
The raw file stays untouched.

16
00:00:50,842 --> 00:00:55,577
Every correction lives in a separate, reversible reporting view,

17
00:00:55,627 --> 00:00:59,200
so any number traces back to its source record.

18
00:01:00,450 --> 00:01:03,133
Two records carry a series name that

19
00:01:03,183 --> 00:01:05,345
contradicts their own title.

20
00:01:05,395 --> 00:01:09,053
They're flagged and re-filed in the reporting view,

21
00:01:09,103 --> 00:01:11,200
never silently overwritten.

22
00:01:12,450 --> 00:01:17,116
A printed resource called Gleebook contains the letters e-book.

23
00:01:17,166 --> 00:01:20,808
A naive substring rule grants it digital access.

24
00:01:20,858 --> 00:01:23,200
A word-boundary rule doesn't.

25
00:01:24,450 --> 00:01:27,576
Only then did I compare prices: same series,

26
00:01:27,626 --> 00:01:29,927
type, pack, edition and access.

27
00:01:29,977 --> 00:01:32,595
Only the level is allowed to change.

28
00:01:32,645 --> 00:01:35,200
That gives sixty comparable groups.

29
00:01:36,450 --> 00:01:40,124
Fifty-four of the sixty hold one price at every level.

30
00:01:40,174 --> 00:01:43,166
Four exceptions are cheaper Starter levels.

31
00:01:43,216 --> 00:01:47,200
The other two aren't pricing at all: they're filing errors.

32
00:01:48,450 --> 00:01:51,009
Matched comparisons show more.

33
00:01:51,059 --> 00:01:55,431
Buying a book in two parts costs about fourteen percent

34
00:01:55,481 --> 00:01:59,200
more than the complete volume, at every level.

35
00:02:00,450 --> 00:02:05,039
But list prices alone can't show demand, costs or margins.

36
00:02:05,089 --> 00:02:07,793
Without sales and discount data,

37
00:02:07,843 --> 00:02:11,200
a recommended price would be speculation.

38
00:02:12,450 --> 00:02:15,031
So the result isn't a dashboard.

39
00:02:15,081 --> 00:02:19,185
It's a method: keep the source, recover the structure,

40
00:02:19,235 --> 00:02:23,200
compare like with like, and say what you don't know.

41
00:02:24,450 --> 00:02:26,348
The interactive case study,

42
00:02:26,398 --> 00:02:29,770
the dataset and the Excel workbook are linked below.

43
00:02:29,820 --> 00:02:33,666
Everything in it is invented, and all of it is reproducible.

44
00:02:33,716 --> 00:02:35,200
Thanks for watching.
